WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.260
Rhiannon: Legislation and guidance and
the processes connected with them, are

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:08.250
there to protect and support the rights
of all children and young people and

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:13.159
their families, and also provide support
to those who work with children and

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.950
young people with mental health issues.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:20.290
Leah: Whether in the home, at school
or in a mental health setting, a child

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.350
or young person should never be treated
in a way that is inhumane or degrading.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.840
They should always be protected
from abuse or neglect that can

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.400
cause mental or physical harm.

00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:32.790
Rhiannon: The family and by
extension, family life, should

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.540
also be respected at all times.

00:00:34.859 --> 00:00:38.790
For example, sending a child or young
person to another part of the country

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:43.570
for residential care and support makes it
harder for family visits and maintaining

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.760
those important relationships which
can be distressing for all concerned.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.250
Leah: Wherever possible a young person
should be consulted about their care

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.020
and be involved in the decision making.

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:58.160
If the child is deemed not legally
competent, consent should be obtained

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.510
from someone with parental responsibility,
unless in the case of an emergency.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.990
Rhiannon: Parental responsibility
should be involved in all

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.999
important decision making in
relation to a child, for example:

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.809
- where the child goes to school,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.210
- appointing a guardian in the
event of the loss of a parent,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.180
- consenting to medical treatment

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.560
- and accessing a child’s medical records.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.046
Leah: This is also the case for legal
matters, and religious or cultural

00:01:21.046 --> 00:01:22.771
issues until the child reaches an
age to make their own decisions.

00:01:22.771 --> 00:01:24.207
Let’s now look at how data
is used and protected.

00:01:24.207 --> 00:01:26.990
Rhiannon: Any data held on children
and young adults comes under the

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.700
'data protection principles' and
that information must be used

00:01:30.719 --> 00:01:33.180
fairly, lawfully and transparently.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.730
It should always be up to date, relevant
and limited to only what is necessary.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.890
Leah: Any information no longer needed,
should be then destroyed securely.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.289
For example, childcare providers and
even child minders need to comply with

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:50.900
data protection for every child in their
care, even if that is for a limited time.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.870
Rhiannon: With further advances in
digital footprints, there is now

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.630
stronger legal protection for online
identification markers, location data,

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:04.380
genetic information, biometrics and
more, and there are separate safeguards

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:08.210
for personal data which relate to
criminal convictions and offences.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.880
Leah: The Data Protection Act, means
an individual has the right to know

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.359
what information the government and
other organisations store about them,

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:19.810
including, the right to be informed
about how it is being used, the ability

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.899
to have access to it and have it
corrected or erased when appropriate.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.950
Rhiannon: Children especially, require
specific protection regarding their

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:32.899
data as they may not be fully aware of
the risks or consequences of processing

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.480
personal data or their rights.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.160
Leah: With so much online communication,
children and young people are constantly

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:42.709
putting their data in the public
domain by signing up to the various

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.350
forms of social media and are not
always aware of the consequences.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.829
Rhiannon: Adults with parental
responsibility should endeavour

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.869
to monitor that data is being used
correctly, whist still respecting the

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.730
rights of the child or young person.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.819
Leah: We must remember legislation
and guidance is there to protect

00:02:59.869 --> 00:03:01.350
and should be used accordingly.

